Experiment: Largest Rectangle in Histogram
1. Aim

To implement a C++ program using a stack to find the area of the largest rectangle in a histogram.

2. Theory

A histogram consists of bars of different heights, where each bar has a width of 1. The objective is to find the maximum rectangular area that can be formed using one or more consecutive bars.

The area of a rectangle is calculated using:

Area=Height×Width

A monotonic increasing stack is used to solve this problem efficiently. The stack stores the indices of bars in increasing order of height. When a shorter bar is encountered, the taller bars are removed from the stack, and their maximum possible rectangle areas are calculated.

A zero-height bar is added at the end to ensure that all remaining bars are processed.

3. Algorithm

Initialize an empty stack and set maxArea = 0.

Append a zero-height bar to the array.

Traverse the histogram from left to right.

If the stack is empty or the current bar is taller than or equal to the top bar, push its index.

If the current bar is shorter than the top bar:

Pop the top index.

Calculate the height of the popped bar.

Calculate the width using the current index and the new stack top.

Calculate the rectangle area.

Update maxArea.

Repeat until all bars are processed.

Return maxArea.

4. C++ Program
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int largestRectangleArea(vector<int>& heights) {
        stack<int> st;
        int maxArea = 0;

        // Add a sentinel bar to process remaining elements
        heights.push_back(0);

        for (int i = 0; i < heights.size(); i++) {

            while (!st.empty() &&
                   heights[st.top()] > heights[i]) {

                int h = heights[st.top()];
                st.pop();

                int width;

                if (st.empty()) {
                    width = i;
                } else {
                    width = i - st.top() - 1;
                }

                int area = h * width;
                maxArea = max(maxArea, area);
            }

            st.push(i);
        }

        return maxArea;
    }
};

int main() {
    Solution obj;

    vector<int> heights = {2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3};

    cout << "Largest Rectangle Area: "
         << obj.largestRectangleArea(heights)
         << endl;

    return 0;
}
5. Sample Input
heights = [2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]
6. Sample Output
Largest Rectangle Area: 10
7. Explanation

The largest rectangle is formed by the bars of heights 5 and 6.

Minimum height = 5

Width = 2

Area=5×2=10

Therefore, the largest rectangle area is 10 square units.

8. Complexity Analysis

Operation

	

Complexity




Time Complexity

	

O(n)




Space Complexity

	

O(n)

Each bar is pushed and popped from the stack at most once.

9. Result

The C++ program was implemented successfully to find the largest rectangle area in a histogram using a monotonic stack.

10. Learning Outcomes

After completing this experiment, students will be able to:

Understand the concept of histograms and rectangular areas.

Implement stacks in C++.

Apply monotonic stack techniques to solve array problems.

Analyze time and space complexity.

Solve the largest rectangle in a histogram efficiently.
